{"name":"Charcoal Sketch","hex":"#5d5b56","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"charcoal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["impure carbon obtained by destructive distillation of wood or other organic matter, that is, heating it in the absence of oxygen.","A stick of black carbon material used for drawing."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark gray colour.","Made of charcoal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/charcoal"},{"term":"sketch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a brief, basic drawing.","To describe briefly and with very few details."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rapidly executed freehand drawing that is not intended as a finished work, often consisting of a multitude of overlapping lines.","A rough design, plan, or draft, as a rough draft of a book."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sketch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sketch (drawing)","blocks":[{"text":"A sketch is a rapidly executed freehand drawing that is not usually intended as a finished work. A sketch may serve a number of purposes: it might record something that the artist sees, it might record or develop an idea for later use or it might be used as a quick way of graphically demonstrating an image, idea or principle. Sketching is the most inexpensive art medium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Jesus_und_Ehebrecherin.jpg/330px-Jesus_und_Ehebrecherin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sketch_(drawing)","wikidata":"Q5078274"},{"title":"Charcoal","blocks":[{"text":"Charcoal is a lightweight black residue made of carbon that is produced by strongly heating wood in minimal oxygen to remove all water and volatile constituents. In the traditional version of this pyrolysis process, called charcoal burning, often by forming a charcoal kiln, the heat is supplied by burning part of the starting material itself, with a limited supply of oxygen. The material can also be heated in a closed retort. Modern charcoal briquettes used for outdoor cooking may contain many other additives, e.g. coal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Charcoal_8.jpg/330px-Charcoal_8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charcoal","wikidata":"Q177463"},{"title":"Shades of gray","blocks":[{"text":"Variations of gray or grey include achromatic grayscale shades, which lie exactly between white and black, and nearby colors with low colorfulness. A selection of a number of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Shades_of_Gray.svg/330px-Shades_of_Gray.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_gray","wikidata":"Q3958716"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Study_of_willows_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg/500px-Study_of_willows_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Study_of_willows_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg/500px-Study_of_willows_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Charcoal sketch of willows by Thomas Gainsborough","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStudy_of_willows_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Artist%27s_Gridded_Sketch_of_Senenmut_MET_36.3.252_EGDP013666.jpg/250px-Artist%27s_Gridded_Sketch_of_Senenmut_MET_36.3.252_EGDP013666.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Artist%27s_Gridded_Sketch_of_Senenmut_MET_36.3.252_EGDP013666.jpg/330px-Artist%27s_Gridd